```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: fjall maintainers fixing recovery logic; fjall users (brk/atproto, embedded Rust KV adopters) who use PersistMode::SyncAll and need to understand durability guarantees after crashes. Specifically when journal framing corruption occurs (torn writes, missing end markers) during crash recovery.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real correctness bug, not microbenchmark artifact. The location-asymmetry (framing vs in-data corruption) is source-verified in fjall 3.1.4 (writer.rs:232, recovery routes). However severity is capped: demonstrated real crash (dm-flakey) only drops UN-fsync'd Buffer-mode data which is documented lossy behavior. The SyncAll-acked data loss is shown only via faithful reorder-sim on real journal bytes, NOT yet via real post-fsync device reordering (dm-log-writes barrier-reorder replay not achieved). For production systems using SyncAll on barrier-honoring storage, the risk remains theoretical until L1 proves post-fsync hole creation.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [fjall is young with limited adoption (brk/atproto cited, no Jepsen/CVE/paper); users have mature alternatives (RocksDB, SQLite, sled); Buffer-mode users already accept documented data loss so silent vs loud may not change behavior; SyncAll users need L1 proof that post-fsync reordering occurs in practice to justify urgency; fix requires changing recovery from silent truncate to loud error which may break existing workflows expecting clean open after crashes]
RATIONALE: Packet shows source-verified asymmetry: in-DATA corruption -> Err(ChecksumMismatch) (50 keys, 0/50 recovered, loud); 5 framing modes -> all open clean with 2-49/50 keys silently discarded, no Err (L0 RESULT). Real dm-flakey crash confirms silent loss for Buffer-mode (documented lossy). Reorder-sim demonstrates 12 SyncAll-acked keys physically present after hole are discarded (38/50 recovered, ERR=none). However packet explicitly states "NOT yet achieved: a REAL reordered DEVICE write dropping data whose SyncAll fsync ALREADY COMPLETED" (Stress-test B). Without L1, the finding is a genuine code bug with capped operational impact—actionable guidance to fix recovery logic or avoid Buffer mode, but not yet a production-critical durability violation for SyncAll users on standard hardware.
```
